READ THIS FIRST

This document is a DRAFT written to record the copyright holder's intended
licensing policy. It has not been reviewed or approved by a lawyer, it is not a
statement that its terms are enforceable in any jurisdiction, and it is not
legal advice. Until a reviewed version replaces it, no one should rely on this
draft as a settled legal position. If you need to rely on these terms for a
commercial arrangement, ask the copyright holder for written permission (see
section 5), which is separate from this draft.

VOLDBASE is SOURCE-AVAILABLE, not open source. The source is published so that
people can read, study, learn from and improve it for non-commercial purposes.
It is not published under an OSI-approved license, and nothing in this document
grants the rights that an open-source license such as the MIT License grants.

1. DEFINITIONS

1.1 "Software" means the source code, documentation, tests, configuration and
    other files in this repository that are covered by this license under
    section 3, together with any part of them.

1.2 "Copyright Holder" means Taqiyeddin Maatlia, the copyright holder of the
    Software, and any successor that holds the copyright in the Software. The
    GitHub account name "Zakariaika" is a handle used by the same person. It is
    not a separate author, copyright holder or legal person, and references to
    it in this repository's earlier files, commit metadata or package metadata
    identify that same person.

1.3 "Non-Commercial Use" means use that is not directed toward or intended for
    commercial advantage or monetary compensation. It includes personal study
    and hobby projects; classroom teaching and coursework; academic research
    that is neither commissioned nor sponsored by a commercial entity and does
    not deliver a service to third parties; and evaluation of the Software for
    the purpose of deciding whether to request a commercial license, provided
    the evaluation is not put to production or commercial use and its results
    are not delivered to third parties for payment. Section 1.8 states which
    specific cases this draft resolves and which it leaves open; non-profit and
    public-sector internal use is deliberately left open there rather than
    classified here.

1.4 "Commercial Use" means any use that is not Non-Commercial Use, including:

    (a) use by or for a for-profit entity in the operation of its business,
        including purely internal use with no product or revenue attached, and
        use by any entity (including a non-profit, educational institution or
        government body) in an activity that is charged for, generates revenue,
        or is performed under an agreement with a commercial sponsor;
    (b) incorporation into a product, service or internal tool that is sold,
        licensed, subscription-based, ad-supported, or used to deliver services
        to third parties;
    (c) use in paid consulting, contracting, development or support work;
    (d) use to provide a hosted, managed or software-as-a-service offering,
        whether or not a fee is charged, where it is operated by or for an
        entity as part of its activities, except the donation-funded free
        public service that section 1.8(m) leaves open;
    (e) use in training, fine-tuning, benchmarking or evaluating a commercial
        model, product or service;
    (f) accepting payment, sponsorship, advertising revenue or other
        consideration in connection with distributing, operating or supporting
        the Software or a Modification, or using the Software to deliver a paid
        service, where that consideration is commercial as this paragraph
        defines it. Consideration is commercial here when it is given in
        exchange for, or in expectation of, a product, service, influence,
        access, promotion or other benefit, or when it is a sponsorship or
        payment tied to the commercial operation, distribution, support or
        promotion of the Software. Receipt alone does not decide the question:
        a donation or gift is not commercial consideration merely because it
        is received by the person or entity that distributes, operates or
        supports the Software. Section 1.8 states the three donation cases —
        an unconditional personal gift, a donation that funds the free public
        service in section 1.8(m), and a payment or sponsorship given for a
        benefit — and only the third is commercial consideration under this
        paragraph. A donation that funds the free service in section 1.8(m) is
        governed by that paragraph and not by this one;
    (g) use by an employee, contractor or agent in the course of their work for
        an employer or principal, where the use by that employer or principal
        would itself be Commercial Use.

    Classifying a use as Commercial Use does not grant it: Commercial Use still
    requires the prior written permission described in section 5.

1.5 "Modify" means to copy, adapt, translate, arrange, alter or create a
    derivative work of the Software, and "Modification" means any resulting
    work.

1.6 "Distribute" means to provide the Software or a Modification to any other
    person or entity, by any means, including publishing it, uploading it,
    bundling it, or shipping it inside another work. "Distribution" has a
    corresponding meaning.

1.7 "You" means the individual or entity exercising rights under this license.

1.8 Cases this draft classifies, and cases it leaves open. The following list
    states the Copyright Holder's intent for the cases that arise most often. It
    is not a substitute for section 5, and it does not decide a case it marks as
    open.

    Resolved as Non-Commercial Use:

    (a) personal study, hobby and other private use by an individual;
    (b) classroom teaching and coursework at an educational institution, where
        the Software is not itself sold or licensed to students;
    (c) academic research that is not commissioned or sponsored by a commercial
        entity and does not deliver a service to third parties;
    (d) evaluation by any person or entity, including a company, solely to
        decide whether to request a commercial license, provided the Software is
        not put to production or commercial use during the evaluation and no
        result is delivered to a third party for payment.

    Resolved as Commercial Use:

    (e) use by a for-profit entity in its business, including purely internal
        use;
    (f) use by an employee, contractor or agent in the course of work for an
        employer or principal whose use would be Commercial Use;
    (g) any hosted, managed or software-as-a-service offering operated by or for
        an entity as part of its activities, whether or not a fee is charged,
        other than the donation-funded case that paragraph (m) leaves open;
    (h) commercial deployment, selling, sublicensing and commercial
        redistribution, each of which also requires permission under section 5;
    (i) accepting payment, sponsorship, advertising revenue or other commercial
        consideration, as section 1.4(f) defines it, in connection with
        distributing, operating or supporting the Software or a Modification,
        other than the cases in paragraphs (m) and (n).

    Left open; not classified by this draft:

    (j) internal use by a non-profit organisation that charges nothing for the
        use and derives no revenue from it;
    (k) internal use by a government body, public authority, public university
        or public research institute;
    (l) academic research with mixed commercial and public funding, or research
        performed under an agreement that gives a commercial sponsor rights in
        the results;
    (m) a donation-funded project that operates the Software as a free public
        service, where access is free of charge and the project derives no
        revenue from the service other than donations that fund the free
        service itself. Such a donation is not consideration for the service
        where it buys no access, priority, promotion, support or other benefit
        and is not a payment or sponsorship tied to commercial operation.
        Section 1.4(d) does not classify this case, section 1.4(f) does not
        treat those donations as consideration, and this draft leaves the case
        open rather than deciding it;
    (n) whether an unconditional personal gift, or a donation that funds the
        free service in paragraph (m), is itself a Commercial Use in any wider
        sense. Paragraph (i) does not classify either case. The three donation
        cases are distinguished below, and this draft decides only the third.

    Donations, gifts and sponsorship are not one case. This draft distinguishes
    three, and classifies only the third:

    Case 1 — an unconditional personal gift or donation, given with no benefit
    promised or expected in return and not connected with distributing,
    operating or supporting the Software. Nothing is exchanged for it and it
    buys no product, service, influence, access, promotion or other benefit, so
    it is not commercial consideration under section 1.4(f). Whether accepting
    such a gift is itself a Commercial Use in a wider sense is left open under
    paragraph (n);

    Case 2 — a donation that funds the free public service in paragraph (m),
    where access remains free and the donation buys no access, priority,
    promotion, support or other benefit. It is not consideration for the
    service, and paragraph (m) governs it; the case is left open with that
    paragraph;

    Case 3 — a payment, sponsorship, advertising arrangement or donation tied
    to the commercial operation, distribution, support, access, promotion or
    other benefit connected with the Software or a Modification, or given in
    exchange for, or in expectation of, any such benefit. This is commercial
    consideration under section 1.4(f) and is Commercial Use under paragraph
    (i).

    Receipt by a project operator, maintainer or contributor does not by itself
    make a donation commercial consideration. What decides Case 3 is the
    benefit given or promised, or the tie to commercial operation, not who
    receives the money.

    For an open case this draft does not choose a classification. Pending the
    Copyright Holder's written decision, an open case should be treated as
    Commercial Use, and a person who needs certainty must request written
    permission under section 5. These cases are repeated in `NOTICE` ("Open
    questions for legal review") and are not presented as settled law.

