6. CONDITIONS ON ALL PERMITTED USE

6.1 You must keep every copyright notice, this license, `NOTICE` and
    `TRADEMARKS.md` with every copy of the Software you make or distribute, and
    must not remove, obscure or alter them.

6.2 You must not represent the Software as your own work, must not remove
    attribution, and must not state or imply that the Copyright Holder endorses
    you, your Modification or your use.

6.3 You must not use the Software to violate any law, to infringe anyone's
    rights, or to build a product that misrepresents its verification results.
    VOLDBASE's documented output states what was and was not tested; suppressing
    `INCONCLUSIVE`, `NOT_TESTED` or `BLOCKED` outcomes, or presenting an
    unexecuted run as a `PASS`, is a material breach of this license.

6.4 You must not circumvent, disable or remove any technical measure in the
    Software that records provenance, integrity or limitations of results.

7. NO EXECUTION CAPABILITY, NO SECURITY GUARANTEE

7.1 The current version of VOLDBASE does not execute imported project code. It
    reads, analyzes and records metadata about projects. It has no execution
    engine, no container runtime and no sandbox, and it does not run a
    project's tests, scripts or build commands.

7.2 VOLDBASE does not provide a validated security guarantee or certification.
    No independent third-party security audit, penetration test or formal
    verification of the Software has been performed. The repository does contain
    internal security design work, threat models and automated checks (for
    example, tests asserting that product code does not import
    `node:child_process`); those are development artifacts, not an audit, and
    their presence is not evidence that the Software is secure. Documentation
    describing future execution isolation states requirements, not capabilities.
    Do not treat any part of this repository as a claim that running untrusted
    code with VOLDBASE is safe.

7.3 You are responsible for the security of the environment in which you run the
    Software and for any project data you point it at.

8. CONTRIBUTIONS

8.1 Non-commercial contributions are welcome and are accepted only under the
    terms of this section (the "Contribution Terms"). These terms apply to a
    contribution only when the contributor has recorded an explicit, affirmative
    acceptance of them through the procedure in `CONTRIBUTING.md`. Submitting a
    pull request, opening an issue, attaching a patch or pushing a commit does
    not by itself record that acceptance, and no such act is treated as
    agreement to these terms. A contribution without recorded acceptance is not
    accepted into the Software: it must not be merged, and if it is merged by
    mistake it may be reverted. The Copyright Holder may also agree separate
    written terms with a contributor, in which case those terms govern that
    contribution to the extent they differ. Where a contribution is owned or
    controlled by an employer, client or other organisation rather than by the
    individual submitting it, acceptance also requires that the organisation's
    authority to submit it and to grant the license in section 8.4 be recorded,
    either in the contributor's acceptance statement or in separate written
    terms under this section.

    An acceptance recorded for a pull request covers the commits in that pull
    request when the statement was posted, and any further commits the
    contributor pushes to the same pull request before it is merged, provided
    they form part of the same contribution. If the contribution changes
    materially before merge, a maintainer may require the contributor to confirm
    the acceptance again. A new pull request requires a new statement.

8.2 When you record acceptance of these Contribution Terms under section 8.1,
    you confirm, for every contribution you submit under them (a pull request,
    patch, issue attachment or any other material intended for inclusion), that:

    (a) you are the author of the contribution or have the right to submit it;
    (b) the contribution is your original work, or any third-party material in
        it is clearly identified, properly licensed, and licensed in a way that
        permits the grant in section 8.4, with its notices preserved;
    (c) to your knowledge the contribution does not infringe anyone's rights;
    (d) if the contribution is owned or controlled by an employer, client or
        other organisation, or is submitted on behalf of one, you have that
        organisation's authority to submit it and to grant the license in
        section 8.4, or that organisation has separately agreed terms in
        writing with the Copyright Holder; and
    (e) you hold, or are authorised to grant, the rights in section 8.4 for the
        whole contribution, and you grant only the rights you actually hold or
        are authorised to grant.

8.3 No transfer of ownership. Nothing in this license transfers, assigns or
    otherwise conveys ownership of any contribution to the Copyright Holder,
    and the Copyright Holder does not claim ownership of any contribution.
    Copyright in a contribution stays with whoever actually holds it: a
    contributor retains the rights the contributor owns, and an employer,
    client or other organisation that owns or controls the contribution retains
    those rights. This section does not assert that a contributor owns
    copyright in every contribution, and it does not decide who owns a
    contribution; it records only that no ownership is transferred by this
    license. The project receives only the license expressly granted in section
    8.4, to the extent the grantor holds, or is authorised to grant, the rights
    in it, and nothing here implies an ownership transfer from any holder.

8.4 License granted to the Copyright Holder. When you record acceptance under
    section 8.1, you grant the Copyright Holder, for each contribution you
    submit under these Contribution Terms and to the extent you hold, or are
    authorised to grant, the rights in it, a perpetual, irrevocable, worldwide,
    royalty-free, non-exclusive, transferable and sublicensable license to:

    (a) reproduce, store and archive the contribution;
    (b) modify it, adapt it and create derivative works of it;
    (c) distribute it, publicly display it and make it available to the public,
        in source or built form, as part of the Software or a derivative of the
        Software;
    (d) sublicense these rights through multiple tiers; and
    (e) license the contribution, as part of the Software, under this license
        or under any other terms, including commercial, paid or otherwise
        restricted terms, and including a future license that replaces this
        draft.

    This is the permission the project needs in order to maintain, distribute
    and commercially license VOLDBASE, and it is granted only for contributions
    you submit under these Contribution Terms or under separate written terms.
    It is a license, not an assignment: it does not make the Copyright Holder
    the owner of your contribution. The grant covers only rights you hold or
    are authorised to grant. It does not cover third-party material you are not
    entitled to license, and it conveys nothing in material that section 8.2(b)
    requires you to identify as owned by another party, beyond what that
    party's license permits.

    Paragraph (e) is the commercial element, and it is deliberate rather than
    incidental: section 5 makes commercial use available only by written
    permission, so the Copyright Holder can grant that permission only if the
    contribution can be offered to the licensee under commercial terms. The
    grant is limited to your contribution as part of the Software. It gives no
    right in your other work, in your name or likeness, or in any project of
    yours outside the Software, and it does not oblige you to provide support,
    maintenance or further contributions.

8.5 You grant every recipient of the Software, as part of the Software and on
    the conditions in this license, only the non-commercial permissions in
    section 4.1 in your contribution. You do not grant recipients the rights in
    section 8.4 — in particular the right to relicense under commercial or other
    terms — which run to the Copyright Holder alone. You do not grant any
    recipient a commercial right, and nothing in this section lets a recipient
    or a downstream fork maintainer grant one. The recipient's permission in
    your contribution reaches them directly from you and the Copyright Holder as
    described in section 4.4, and it covers your contribution only as part of
    the Software.

8.6 No contribution creates an employment, agency, partnership or fiduciary
    relationship, and no contribution entitles you to compensation, revenue
    share or ownership in the project.

8.7 The Copyright Holder is not obliged to accept, review, merge or keep any
    contribution, and may remove a contribution at any time. Removing a
    contribution does not revoke the license granted in section 8.4 for the
    period during which it was part of the Software.

8.8 Contributions are provided "as is", without warranty of any kind, and the
    contributor is not liable for their contribution except to the extent the
    law does not allow that limitation.

8.9 Moral rights. To the extent your jurisdiction allows it, you consent to the
    modification, combination and distribution of your contribution under this
    license without further attribution beyond the notices kept with the
    Software, and you agree not to assert moral rights in a way that prevents
    the Copyright Holder from exercising the license in section 8.4. If your
    jurisdiction does not allow that consent, tell the Copyright Holder before
    submitting, because the project cannot accept a contribution it is not able
    to license as described.

8.10 Recording acceptance. The procedure is set out in `CONTRIBUTING.md`.
     Acceptance is recorded in the pull request or patch record in a form that
     identifies the contributor and the date, and that record is kept with the
     project's records for as long as the contribution remains in the Software.
     The project does not infer acceptance from silence, from a commit, or from
     the act of opening a pull request, and a maintainer must not merge a
     contribution that does not carry it. If a contributor cannot use the
     repository's pull request interface, the same statement may be given to
     the Copyright Holder in writing and recorded with the contribution.

     The recorded statement identifies the contributor by the name or handle
     they give. It does not verify their legal identity, their authorship or
     ownership of the contribution, or their authority to license it, and the
     project does not treat a GitHub account, an email address or a commit
     author field as proof of any of those things. Whether the procedure binds a
     particular contributor, and whether additional evidence would be needed to
     rely on it, is a question for counsel (`NOTICE`, "Open questions for legal
     review").

8.11 A documented procedure, not a legal conclusion. Section 8.1 and
     `CONTRIBUTING.md` describe how the project records consent. They are not a
     claim that the procedure is legally sufficient in any jurisdiction, that it
     binds a contributor in every circumstance, or that a lawyer has approved it.
     That question is recorded in `NOTICE` ("Open questions for legal review").

