11. TERM AND TERMINATION

11.1 This license takes effect when you first exercise a permission it grants
     and continues until terminated under this section.

11.2 Material breach and notice. If you materially breach this license, the
     Copyright Holder may terminate your permission only by written notice that
     identifies the breach and states what is required to address it. A breach
     is material only if it defeats the purpose of this license. Commercial use
     or commercial distribution without permission under section 5, and
     presenting an unexecuted run as a `PASS` in breach of section 6.3, are
     material. A failure to keep the notices required by section 6.1 is
     material only where it defeats that purpose — for example, removing
     notices in order to pass the Software off as unlicensed or as the
     distributor's own work — and not where it is an accidental or technical
     omission corrected after notice.

     This draft distinguishes three kinds of material breach. It does not treat
     a breach as incurable, and so immediately terminable, merely because
     something that has already happened cannot be reversed:

     (a) Curable breach. The breach can be fully cured — for example, a
         missing notice can be restored, or an unauthorised use can be stopped
         and its copies withdrawn before any further distribution. The notice
         states what is required to cure it. Termination takes effect 30 days
         after the notice unless, before then, you fully cure the breach and
         confirm the cure in writing to the Copyright Holder; a full cure
         within that period means no termination occurs for that breach;

     (b) Breach whose continuing effects can be stopped or mitigated, but whose
         past occurrence cannot literally be undone — for example, an
         unauthorised distribution that has already reached recipients. The
         notice states the steps reasonably required to stop or mitigate the
         continuing effects. Termination takes effect 30 days after the notice
         unless those steps are taken and confirmed in writing before then.
         Taking them prevents termination for that breach, but does not undo
         the breach, does not make the past occurrence lawful, and does not
         limit any remedy the Copyright Holder has for it;

     (c) Genuinely incurable breach. Neither the breach nor its continuing
         effects can be stopped or mitigated. This paragraph applies only where
         neither can be stopped or mitigated; where either can be, the breach
         falls under paragraph (a) or (b). Termination takes effect when the
         notice is given.

     Termination under this section is never automatic. Whether taking the
     steps described in paragraph (b) amounts to a cure, whether that paragraph
     is the right construction in a given legal system, and how a breach is
     allocated among paragraphs (a) to (c), are questions for qualified
     counsel; they are recorded in `NOTICE` ("Open questions for legal
     review") rather than settled here.

11.3 Minor, technical and accidental breaches. Termination is not automatic
     and is not available for a breach that is not material. An accidental or
     technical violation — including an inadvertent omission of a notice, a
     metadata error, or a momentary failure that is corrected promptly after the
     Copyright Holder points it out — does not terminate this license and does
     not by itself become material. The Copyright Holder may require you to
     correct it. Only if you fail to correct it after written notice, and that
     continued failure defeats the purpose of this license, may it become a
     material breach under section 11.2. A breach does not move into paragraph
     (b) or (c) of section 11.2 merely because correcting it cannot erase what
     has already happened; the question under this section is whether it was
     material in the first place. Nothing in this section creates a termination
     right that section 11.2 does not give.

11.4 Claims and challenges. Bringing a claim that the Copyright Holder's rights
     in the Software are invalid, or that the Copyright Holder's exercise of
     those rights infringes your rights, does not by itself terminate this
     license. The Copyright Holder may terminate only under section 11.2.
     Nothing in this license requires you to give up a right to bring or defend
     a claim, and nothing in it prevents the Copyright Holder from defending
     one.

11.5 Effect of termination. On termination you must stop all use and
     Distribution of the Software and of any Modification and delete the copies
     you hold, except copies you are required by law to retain and copies you
     have already lawfully distributed to recipients who remain in compliance
     with this license.

11.6 What termination does not do. Termination does not revoke a permission
     already granted to a recipient who received a copy under section 4.1 or
     section 4.4 and remains in compliance; it does not revoke any right that
     applicable law does not allow to be revoked; it does not affect a
     permission the Copyright Holder has separately granted in writing; and it
     does not limit any remedy the Copyright Holder has for a breach that
     occurred before termination.

11.7 Reinstatement. A person whose permission has terminated may ask the
     Copyright Holder in writing for reinstatement. Reinstatement is at the
     Copyright Holder's discretion, takes effect only in writing, and does not
     excuse a breach that occurred before it.

11.8 Sections 1, 3, 6.2, 7, 8.3, 8.4, 8.5, 8.9, 9, 10, 12, 13, 14 and this
     section survive termination.

     Survival preserves the terms, not a terminated party's permission. What a
     terminated party and its recipients may still do is governed by sections
     11.5 and 11.6. Section 8.3 survives because the copyright held by a
     contributor or any other rights holder is not affected by the termination
     of any license granted under this document. Section 8.9 survives only so
     that a contribution already part of a copy lawfully distributed before
     termination may continue to be distributed under the permissions that
     survive under section 11.6; it does not give a terminated party any new
     right to modify or distribute the Software.

